  <section class="py-5 bg-white">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="col-12 col-lg-10 mx-auto">
        <h2 class="heading-border heading-center mb-4 pb-3 d-inline-block">What the Platform Does, Feature by Feature</h2>
        <div class="row g-4">
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">Web Booking Portal</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                Patients register with MFA, see provider availability in a calendar view, and book, reschedule, or cancel appointments. An AI chat interface lets patients describe what they need in plain language ("I need a cleaning with Dr. Patel next Tuesday") and the system books it. The AI chat runs on Claude with a PHI de-identification layer, patient identifiers are stripped before any text reaches the model and re-associated server-side after. Claude never sees a name, date of birth, or phone number.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">Voice Booking Agent</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                Patients call the clinic's phone number and speak naturally. Twilio streams the audio to Deepgram Nova-2 for real-time transcription. A state machine dialogue manager processes the transcript, runs intent detection through the same Claude PHI de-id pipeline used by the web booking, queries available slots, and responds via ElevenLabs TTS. The agent handles booking, rescheduling, cancellations, and general inquiries (hours, location, insurance). At any point, the patient can say "speak to a person" and get a warm transfer with full context summary. After-hours calls route to a callback queue visible to staff the next morning. The target was under 2 seconds round-trip latency; the production system achieved sub-2-second response time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">Provider Dashboard</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                Providers see a color-coded daily schedule timeline. They click appointments to update statuses (arrived, in-progress, no-show, completed). Each status change is audit-logged. Providers manage their own availability templates and block dates. The dashboard also surfaces the scribe interface for documentation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">AI Medical Scribe</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                The most clinically impactful feature. The provider selects the patient, confirms consent, and taps Record. The scribe uses Deepgram's diarization mode to separate the provider's voice from the patient's voice in real time. When the provider taps Stop, the system runs the transcript through Claude with a strict system prompt: extract a SOAP note (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) from the conversation, never fabricate findings absent from the transcript, mark gaps as [Not discussed], mark uncertain items as [VERIFY], and suggest ICD-10 codes validated against the CMS code table. The SOAP note appears in seconds. The provider reviews, edits if needed, approves, and pushes directly to athenahealth via FHIR API. Audio is never stored, chunks stream to Deepgram and are discarded. Only the text transcript persists, encrypted.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">Athenahealth Integration</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                Bi-directional FHIR R4 sync: provider schedules and patient records pull from athenahealth on connect. Every booking made on the platform pushes immediately to athenahealth. A batch sync runs every 10 minutes to catch changes made directly in athenahealth (cancellations, schedule changes). Conflicts are detected and surfaced to the admin. An EMRIntegration base class means adding a second EHR (Epic, Cerner) requires implementing a new adapter, not rebuilding the integration layer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">Admin Panel</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                Clinic configuration, user management, RBAC, booking rules, audit log viewer, voice call logs with transcripts, call analytics (resolution rate, escalation rate, average handle time), and integration status. The audit log is append-only, no UPDATE or DELETE permissions for any role. Immutable by design.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">HIPAA Compliance Architecture</h3>
              <p class="mb-2 small">Every layer of the platform was built with HIPAA controls from day one, not added later:</p>
              <ul class="mb-0 ps-3 small" style="line-height: 1.8;">
                <li>PHI encryption at rest using pgcrypto field-level encryption on all PHI columns</li>
                <li>TLS on all endpoints, HTTPS enforced</li>
                <li>JWT authentication with 15-minute access tokens and 7-day refresh tokens</li>
                <li>TOTP MFA for all staff accounts</li>
                <li>RBAC middleware: every endpoint declares required role + permission, checked before the handler runs</li>
                <li>Session inactivity timeout (15 minutes), account lockout after 5 failed attempts</li>
                <li>Row Level Security on all tables (multi-tenant isolation at the database layer)</li>
                <li>PHI de-identification pipeline: all text going to Claude is stripped of patient identifiers, with encrypted token mapping stored server-side</li>
                <li>Audit logging on every API call that touches PHI: who, when, what resource, what action, IP, result</li>
                <li>Audit log is append-only (no UPDATE or DELETE permissions)</li>
                <li>Audio non-persistence: scribe audio chunks stream to Deepgram and are discarded; only text persists</li>
                <li>BAA-covered infrastructure for Twilio, Deepgram, ElevenLabs, and hosting provider</li>
                <li>Break Glass emergency access: elevated access requires mandatory reason entry, auto-revokes after 1 hour, immediately alerts admin, fully audit-trailed</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

