  <section class="py-5 bg-white">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="col-12 col-lg-10 mx-auto">
        <h2 class="heading-border heading-center mb-4 pb-3 d-inline-block">Engineering Deep Dive</h2>
        <div class="row g-4">
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">PHI De-Identification Pipeline</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                The core safety mechanism for all AI use in the platform. Before any patient text reaches Claude, whether from the voice booking dialogue or the scribe transcript, a de-identification module scans the input for patient identifiers: names matched against the patient database, DOB patterns, SSN regex, MRN format, phone numbers, email addresses, and physical addresses. Each detected identifier is replaced with an opaque token ([TOKEN-A], [TOKEN-B]). The token mapping is stored in an encrypted server-side session and never transmitted. After Claude returns structured output, tokens are re-associated server-side. Every Claude API call is logged, what de-identified text was sent, what was returned, and what PHI was stripped. The audit trail proves PHI never reached the model. The pipeline is intentionally conservative: a false positive (stripping non-PHI) is harmless. A false negative (sending PHI to Claude) is a HIPAA violation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">Voice Agent Latency Architecture</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                The original requirement was under 2 seconds round-trip for a natural conversation feel. The production system achieved this through four specific decisions: streaming Deepgram interim transcripts for early intent detection before the caller finishes speaking; keeping a warm connection to Claude rather than cold-starting per request; pre-caching responses for the five most common intents (hours, location, insurance, "I want to book", "I want to cancel") so they never hit Claude at all; and routing simple queries (hours, location, general info) to tenant configuration data directly, bypassing the AI pipeline entirely. Barge-in detection was implemented so the agent stops speaking if the caller interrupts, critical for natural conversation feel.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">Scheduling Concurrency and Double-Booking Prevention</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                The number one scheduling bug in high-volume systems is two patients booking the same slot concurrently. The platform uses SELECT ... FOR UPDATE at the database level in the booking transaction, a row-level lock that prevents any concurrent booking of the same slot from completing until the first transaction either commits or rolls back. This is tested explicitly: a script that sends 10 concurrent booking requests for the same slot. Only one must succeed. Reschedule operations are atomic: the old slot is freed and the new slot is booked in a single transaction. If either fails, both roll back.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
            <div class="workflow-card p-4 h-100">
              <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted mb-3">AI Medical Scribe Hallucination Prevention</h3>
              <p class="mb-0 small">
                A SOAP note that fabricates findings that were not said in the conversation is a clinical safety risk. The Claude prompt for the scribe includes explicit fabrication prohibition: mark missing information as [Not discussed], mark uncertain items as [VERIFY], never infer clinical findings from context. ICD-10 suggestions are validated against the CMS local lookup table before display, invalid codes are stripped before the provider sees them. Every generated note displays a persistent "AI-Generated, Please Review Before Saving" banner. Provider approval is required before any note goes to athenahealth. The system was accuracy-tested against 15+ real recordings targeting 90%+ completeness and zero fabrication.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

